{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And it came.</b> The angel." "And the cloud and the darkness were between camp and camp." "<b>And it illuminated the night.</b> According to the rule of all nights for Israel to cross the sea, for they crossed at night. And R. Marinus said that the meaning of ויאר את הלילה is 'and it darkened,' and similar to it: ולילה אור בעדני (Psalms 139:11), and in the words of the Sages: אור לארבעה עשר (Pesahim 2a). And this interpretation sets darkness for light and light for darkness, for it is not possible in any language that the meaning of one word should be a thing and its opposite, unless it is by way of euphemism, like ברך נבות אלהים (1 Kings 21:13). And the word אור בעדני I have explained that it was light in itself; also the Sages said אור לארבעה עשר is an elegant expression — but it is like a euphemism. And what reason to mention 'and it darkened the night,' for every night is dark? Rather, ויאר את הלילה, as He did from the day Israel went out of Egypt. For the pillar of fire was every night, and if they had no light, how would they cross the sea? For Pharaoh came into the sea, and HaShem looked upon him in the morning watch, and most of Israel had already crossed."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:34:31.152022573Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}